We will show that if $d< rac{rk}{r-k}-1$, then for any $r$-partition $(C_1,\ldots,C_r)$ of $X$, some $k$ of the convex hulls $\conv C_1,\ldots,\conv C_r$ have empty intersection. We proceed by induction on $r$.  This will enable us to focus on partitions $(C_1,\dots, C_r)$ of $X$ where each part $C_j$ misses at least one ray $R_i$. For $r =2$ there is nothing to prove.  Now assume $r > 2$, and suppose the theorem holds for $r-1$.  Let $(C_1,\dots, C_r)$ be an $r$-partition of the set $X$ defined above.  If one of the parts, say $C_r$, contains a point from each ray $R_i$, then we turn to the induction hypothesis.  We delete $C_r$, define $	ilde X = X \smallsetminus C_r$, and consider the $(r-1)$-partition $(C_1,\dots, C_{r-1})$ of $	ilde X$.  Note that $	ilde X$ contains at most $r-2$ points on each ray $R_i$ and is in "general position", like $X$. If $2\le k < r-1$, apply the induction hypothesis:  By assumption, $d <  rac{rk}{r-k} - 1$ and since $ rac{rk}{r-k} -1 <  rac{(r-1)k}{r-1-k} - 1, \; 	ilde X$ satisfies  the conditions of the theorem, and therefore some $k$ of the convex hulls $ \conv C_j (j = 1, \dots, r-1)$ have empty intersection. If $k = r-1$, then $ igcap^{r-1}_{j=1}  \conv C_j= \emptyset$.  Indeed if $x \in igcap^{r-1}_{j=1}  \conv C_j$, then $\supp x \subset  igcap^{r-1}_{j=1} I(C_j)$ by Proposition $ef{prop:supp_x}$.  But $ igcap^{r-1}_{j=1} I(C_j) = \emptyset$, since each ray $R_i$ is missed by at least one of the parts $C_1,\dots, C_{r-1}$.  The only point $x\in br^d$ with $\supp x = \emptyset$ is the origin $\underbar{0} $, but $\underbar{0} 
otin  \conv C_j$ unless $I(C_j) = D$. From now on we assume that for every $j$, $I(C_j)\subsetneq D$. We now prove the theorem. To do this we define a (weight) function: given $k$ distinct parts (say $C_{j_1},\ldots,C_{j_k}$) and  a ray $R_i$,  define:  egin{multline*} W((C_{j_1},\ldots,C_{j_k}),R_i)  := \left\{ egin{array}{l} 0\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\, 	ext{if }\ R_i \cap C_{j_s}=\emptyset 	ext{ for some } s \in \{1,\ldots,k\}\ \ 1+\#\{s:|C_{j_s}\cap R_i|>1\}\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\, 	ext{otherwise}\, . \end{array}ight.\end{multline*} In Section $ef{subsec:Lower_Bound}$ we will show that if $\cap_{s=1}^k \conv C_{j_s}
eq \emptyset$ then  egin{equation} \sum_{i=0}^d W((C_{j_1},\ldots,C_{j_k}),R_i)\geq k. \end{equation} In Section $ef{subsec:Upper_Bound}$ we will show that for each $i\in D$:  egin{equation}\sum_{1\leq j_1<j_2<\ldots<j_k\leq r}W((C_{j_1},\ldots,C_{j_k}),R_i)\leq  inom{r-1}{k}. \end{equation} We use these two results to establish Theorem $ef{thm:secondThm}$.  If $\cap_{s=1}^k \conv C_{j_s}
eq \emptyset$ for all $1\leq j_1<j_2<\ldots<j_k\leq r$, then from the inequalities ($ef{eq:buba}$) and ($ef{eq:baba}$) we conclude:  egin{equation}  k inom{r}{k}\leq \sum_{1\leq j_1<j_2<\ldots<j_k\leq r}\sum_{i=0}^d W((C_{j_1},\cdots,C_{j_k}),R_i)\leq (d+1) inom{r-1}{k}.\end{equation} We thus obtain: \[k inom{r}{k}\leq (d+1) inom{r-1}{k} \] which is equivalent to $d\geq  rac{rk}{r-k}-1$, and the theorem follows.
